### What does volatile guarantee?

volatile provides two guarantees: visibility and ordering. It does not provide atomicity.

### How does visibility work without volatile?

Without volatile, the JIT compiler may optimize a variable read into a CPU register. The thread reads the register repeatedly and never checks main memory.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class VisibilityProblem {
    private static boolean running = true; // NOT volatile

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            // JIT may hoist: if (running) while(true) { ... }
            while (running) {
                // busy work
            }
            System.out.println("Worker stopped");
        });
        worker.start();
        Thread.sleep(1000);
        running = false; // written to main memory, but worker may never see it
        System.out.println("Main set running=false");
        worker.join(2000);
        if (worker.isAlive()) {
            System.out.println("Worker is STILL running! Visibility bug.");
        }
    }
}
```

### How does volatile fix the visibility problem?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class VisibilityFix {
    private static volatile boolean running = true; // volatile!

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            while (running) { // volatile read: checks main memory each time
                // busy work
            }
            System.out.println("Worker stopped");
        });
        worker.start();
        Thread.sleep(1000);
        running = false; // volatile write: flushes to main memory
        worker.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Worker stopped
```

### Why does volatile NOT make counter++ atomic?

`counter++` is three separate operations: read the value, increment it, and write it back. Volatile ensures each individual read or write accesses main memory, but two threads can still interleave between steps.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class VolatileCounterRace {
    private static volatile int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter++; // NOT atomic!
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) counter++;
        });
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        System.out.println("Counter: " + counter);
        // Expected: 200000. Actual: less (e.g., 156789)
    }
}
```

```text
Counter: 156789
```

### What is the happens-before relationship?

The happens-before relationship guarantees that all writes before a volatile write are visible to any thread that subsequently reads the volatile variable.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class HappensBeforeDemo {
    private static int data = 0;           // non-volatile
    private static volatile boolean ready = false; // volatile

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread writer = new Thread(() -> {
            data = 42;         // write to non-volatile
            ready = true;      // volatile write: establishes happens-before
        });

        Thread reader = new Thread(() -> {
            while (!ready) { } // volatile read: sees everything before the write
            System.out.println("Data: " + data); // guaranteed to see 42
        });

        writer.start();
        reader.start();
        writer.join();
        reader.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Data: 42
```

### When should you use volatile?

| Use case | Volatile? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Shutdown flag: `volatile boolean running` | Yes | Single writer, many readers, no compound op |
| Latest price: `volatile BigDecimal price` | Yes | One writer publishes, many readers consume |
| Double-checked locking: `volatile Singleton` | Yes | Prevents seeing partially constructed object |
| Counter: `counter++` | No | Compound operation, use AtomicInteger |
| Balance check+debit | No | Check-then-act, use synchronized |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Memory barriers**: on x86, a volatile write inserts a StoreLoad barrier. On ARM, both LoadLoad and StoreStore barriers are needed. The JVM handles this automatically.
- **volatile vs synchronized**: volatile is lighter (no monitor acquisition) but weaker (no atomicity, no mutual exclusion). synchronized provides all three guarantees.
- **volatile long/double**: on 32-bit JVMs, reading a non-volatile `long` or `double` is not atomic (it is two 32-bit reads). Declaring them `volatile` guarantees atomic read/write. On 64-bit JVMs, this is already atomic in practice.
